This directory contains a Geneva application that uses an external 
program for the setup of individuals and evaluation of candidate
solutions. A sample evaluation program written in Python is available
in the 'evaluator' subdirectory.

The exchange with the program
-----------------------------
The program is called with one step at a time, after the words of
"custom_options" in config/GExternalEvaluatorIndividual.json:

  --init                     once, before the setup
  --setup --output=FILE --initvalues=random|min|max
                             writes the setup document: the variables
                             (type GConstrainedDoubleObject, start value,
                             lowerBoundary, upperBoundary, initRandom),
                             nVars (their number) and n_results
  --evaluate --input=FILE --output=FILE
                             reads one individual, writes its n_results
                             results (rawResult) and isValid
  --archive --input=FILE     receives the best individuals at the end
  --finalize                 once, at the end

All documents are JSON; evaluator/evaluator.py shows each of them.
A setup whose nVars differs from the number of its variables is
refused. If the setup asks for a random start (initRandom, or
"init_values": "random" when initRandom is absent), every individual
starts at random values within the bounds.

Run directories
---------------
Each --setup, --evaluate and --archive exchanges its files in a
directory of its own, <program>-<UUID>, in the working directory; the
file names passed to the program are absolute ("parameter_file" names
the input file of an evaluation). The program itself runs in the
working directory of this application, so it can read files relative
to it. The directory is removed afterwards; with
"remove_exec_temporaries": false it is kept, for debugging. It then
also holds program-output.txt, the program's output.

Time limit
----------
"evaluation_time_limit" (seconds, 0 = none) ends an evaluation that
takes longer: the program gets SIGTERM, and SIGKILL 10 s later; the
evaluation counts as failed. A program that starts further programs
passes the signal on to them, or replaces itself with exec. A failed
evaluation gets the worst fitness, and its message names the command,
how the program ended and the last lines of its output.
